COMPLIANT — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3d is satisfied by the first remedy it names.

The consumer dispatches on the type of each documented shape, so the new behaviour is reachable
from both arms of the union, and an unrecognised shape raises a typed error instead of falling
through to a default. The coverage varies the SHAPE rather than the value and asserts the same
result through both arms, which is what makes a silently-defaulting branch fail rather than pass.

Trap 1 — A STRUCTURAL EXISTENCE PROBE IS PRESENT IN THE SAME FUNCTION AND IS KEPT. It is the
literal construct this clause polices, sitting a few lines below the dispatch. It is over a single
concrete type where the attribute marks an optional capability, not over a union of documented
return shapes, so a false result is the answer to the question being asked rather than a silent
miss. The clause is scoped to consumption of a dual-shape return; a judge keyed on the token will
flag a compliant line.

Trap 2 — the wrapper arm still carries a fallback to a default. It is a null-value fallback within
one known shape, reached only after that shape has been positively identified, not a fallback that
absorbs an unrecognised shape.

Trap 3 — the mutation control is easy to read past: the wrapper arm is replaced with the default
and the case reds, so the assertion could have come back the other way.

Nothing else fires. No documented parameter is dropped, nothing is stubbed, no error is swallowed,
and the commit body describes only what the diff contains.
